# Kernel: resample
# Draws the lifted picture into one destination chunk under an affine map.
#
# The map is handed over already inverted, as a 3 by 3 whose two upper rows are
# `rows` and `shift` and whose bottom row is `persp`, so the CPU composes scale,
# rotation, shear, offset and the perspective once per dispatch run rather than
# once per texel. An affine map has a bottom row of (0, 0, 1), so the divide is by
# one. Perspective is the only thing that puts anything else there.
#
# The source is one texture holding the whole lifted piece rather than the chunks
# it came out of. A bilinear read needs the four texels around a position, and at
# a chunk border two live in the neighbour, which is not bound. Stitching first
# gives a resample with no seam every 256 pixels, at the cost of one texture the
# size of the lifted box for the life of the gesture.
#
# Alpha is premultiplied for the interpolation and divided back out. The read is in
# linear light and averages every texel under a destination texel where the map
# shrinks the picture, `transform_read.gmacs` says how. The footprint is taken
# here, from where the map sends the next texel along each axis, so a perspective
# that shrinks the far side averages more there.
#
# Nearest is one read rather than four. It is what pixel art wants, and the only
# filter that cannot invent a colour the picture did not hold.

kernel resample

image source: readonly
image destination: writeonly

params:
    # Where the dispatch starts inside the destination chunk, and how big it is.
    vec4 dest
    # The document position of the destination chunk's own (0, 0), and the size
    # of the source texture.
    vec4 world
    # The inverse map's two rows.
    vec4 rows
    # Its translation, then which filter to read with, 0 for bilinear and 1
    # for nearest, and one float of padding to close the block.
    vec4 shift
    # The inverse map's third row, which is what makes it a homography
    # rather than an affine: `xy` are the two coefficients on the document
    # position and `z` is the constant. An affine map sends (0, 0, 1) here and
    # the divide below is by one, so nothing that was affine moves by a texel.
    vec4 persp
    # The document's transfer curve, the four numbers `transfer_curve.gmacs`
    # takes, and the identity for a field that is not colour.
    vec4 curve

# The read of the lifted picture, shared with `warp.gmacs`, which puts a
# second map in front of it. Included after the params because it
# reads the `source` image, and an include is text.
import transform_read

# Where the map sends the document position `world_pos`, in the source's
# texels, centre to centre. A position past the horizon is sent nowhere near,
# which only ever widens the footprint of a texel already at the horizon.


def sa_mapped(vec2 world_pos) -> vec2:
    vec2 mapped = vec2(dot(rows.xy, world_pos), dot(rows.zw, world_pos)) + shift.xy
    return mapped / max(dot(persp.xy, world_pos) + persp.z, 1.0e-6) - vec2(0.5)


def resample():
    ivec2 local = ivec2(global_id.xy) + ivec2(dest.xy)
    if local.x >= int(dest.x + dest.z) or local.y >= int(dest.y + dest.w):
        return

    # The texel's centre, because a map applied at the corner shifts the whole
    # picture half a texel up and left at every scale but one.
    vec2 world_pos = vec2(local) + world.xy + vec2(0.5)
    vec2 mapped = vec2(dot(rows.xy, world_pos), dot(rows.zw, world_pos)) + shift.xy
    # The homogeneous divide. A texel whose w is nought or negative is
    # behind the horizon the four corners describe, and there is no colour it
    # could take, so it is left transparent rather than folded back into the
    # picture as a mirrored ghost.
    float w = dot(persp.xy, world_pos) + persp.z
    if w <= 0.0:
        destination[local] = vec4(0.0)
        return
    vec2 at = mapped / w - vec2(0.5)
    vec2 footprint = sa_footprint(sa_mapped(world_pos + vec2(1.0, 0.0)) - at, sa_mapped(world_pos + vec2(0.0, 1.0)) - at)
    destination[local] = sa_read_source(at, ivec2(world.zw), shift.z > 0.5, footprint, curve)
